Diagnosis:
The bug is due to the code between lines 883-885 in `appendFixedWidthPadRight`:

```java
if (strLen >= width) {
    str.getChars(0, strLen, buffer, size);
} else {
    int padLen = width - strLen;
    str.getChars(0, strLen, buffer, size);
    for (int i = 0; i < padLen; i++) {
        buffer[size + strLen + i] = padChar;
    }
}
```

If `strLen >= width` (string length greater or equal to the specified width), the code copies the entire string (length `strLen`) into the buffer starting at `size`. However, the buffer was only ensured to have capacity `size + width`, which is less than `size + strLen`. This causes an overflow or buffer corruption.

The copy operation should only copy `width` number of characters, not `strLen`. This ensures that the copied string fits into the fixed-width space.

In other words, the line:
```java
str.getChars(0, strLen, buffer, size);
```
should be changed to:
```java
str.getChars(0, width, buffer, size);
```

This will prevent copying more characters than the fixed width, thereby fixing the overflow issue causing the test failure.

---

Suggested methods for fix:
- org.apache.commons.lang.text.StrBuilder.appendFixedWidthPadRight(java.lang.Object, int, char)

